00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.740
Today, we'll be
discussing a topic

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.630
that is very popular today.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.080
And we'll have an
opportunity to do a Q&amp;A

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.490
at the end of the webcast.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.830
So let me introduce myself.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:20.840
My name's Joey Wong.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.050
I'm the customer engineer
for Apigee, which

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.520
is a part of Google Cloud.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.960
And with me I have--

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.210
SARTHAK GANGULY: I am Sarthak.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.640
I'm a partner engineer
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.100
And interestingly, I was part
of Apigee until last year.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.550
So I have seen both
sides of the world.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:45.140
And we are joining in from not
sunny but cold in New York.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.570
JOEY WONG: It is
certainly getting colder.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.670
So today, we'll actually be
discussing a couple of items.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:54.080
And really it's about how
organizations are actually

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.140
embarking on a journey and
transition from a traditionally

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.880
on-premise to a hybrid
IT architecture and some

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.730
of those challenges that are
faced in that journey itself.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.550
We'll also present how
Apigee can help de-risk,

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.910
in some cases accelerate
application modernization

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:15.320
and migration by introducing an
API management layer to broker

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.080
transactions between legacy
back-end systems and Cloud

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.400
Services.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.900
So join us as we share
how we can help you

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.480
make that move to the cloud.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.000
So before we get down
into the details itself,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.240
interestingly
enough, Google itself

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.160
conducts a survey
with our customers

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.770
and our customers' CIOs.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.210
Some of the biggest
questions are,

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.210
are they currently using
a multi-cloud strategy?

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.960
And based on our
survey, the response

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.950
has been very eye-opening.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:50.210
Based on the survey
itself, 96% of the CIOs

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.700
and the customers that
we actually talk to today

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.260
have actually either deployed
or are working on deploying

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.100
or are thinking seriously
about a multi-cloud strategy.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:06.185
And the reasons for this are a
number of key points, really.

00:02:06.185 --> 00:02:08.509
It's really about
empowering the organization

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:11.780
to mix and match
solutions and to be

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.910
able to leverage
innovative technologies

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:16.280
from the different cloud
providers themselves.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.810
Every single one
of our customers

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.460
have some unique
requirements, which

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.270
they need to then tailor
their solutions based

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:24.650
on those requirements.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:28.850
And additionally, it's really
to increase redundancy and avoid

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.270
downtime and to ensure that
their business is highly

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:34.430
available and also scalable.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:37.520
And we see this a lot in our
financial services customers

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.710
and also our health
care customers as well.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.210
So as our customers are actually
embarking on this journey

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.630
to cloud, there are
multiple ways of migration.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.540
It's not a single path to cloud.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:55.880
And when we have a look at it,
when they start with legacy

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.490
on premise, there are
actually a number of options

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.020
that they can take.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.408
They can modernize
on premise, where

00:03:02.408 --> 00:03:04.700
they could start taking some
of those applications that

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.940
are monolith applications,
whether they're commercially

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.640
off-the-shelf
applications or something

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.650
that they've built
in-house, and start

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.120
breaking down into
smaller microservices

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.240
and taking that microservices
architecture approach.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.030
When they do that, they also
have the ability to, then,

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:26.270
take those modernized
applications, microservices,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.180
and whatnot and then
move to the cloud.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.370
Now, not every
single application

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.070
can be broken down
into microservices.

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:38.480
So one of the strategies
that our customers take

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.390
is the lift and shift, where
they take existing monolith

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:45.480
applications and then move
it into the cloud itself.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.190
Now, that doesn't necessarily
bring all the advantages

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:50.750
of cloud, naturally.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:53.900
But it does reduce
operational overhead.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.560
It does reduce the cost of
having to procure new hardware.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:01.640
It does reduce the need to
keep up with upgrade cycles,

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.080
patching up the OS and whatnot.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.200
So there are some advantages
of just doing a lift and shift.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.290
And interestingly
enough, it's never

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:13.160
just one way which is more nice,
on prem and then move to cloud

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.500
or lift and shift and
then move to the cloud

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.940
and then modernize.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:18.709
It's usually
somewhere in between,

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:21.920
where within the
same company, even

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.650
within the same
organization, they actually

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:28.850
do both, where they
start modernizing on prem

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:32.420
and also start lifting and
shifting certain workloads

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:33.350
into the cloud.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.570
For folks that aren't
familiar with Apigee,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.980
Apigee is an API
management platform

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.920
which provides the
ability for you

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.640
to create API
proxies and facades,

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.580
which sit in front of
your back-end systems,

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.770
such as cloud applications,
legacy on-premise applications,

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.620
commercially off-the-shelf
applications that

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.250
have been traditionally
deployed on premise.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.340
Now, when we create that
facade through Apigee,

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.950
you get a number
of capabilities,

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:07.070
such as security,
analytics, and visibility

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:10.700
into who is actually now
calling into those APIs

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.150
and back-end systems.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.430
You have the ability
to publish those APIs

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.650
to develop a portal for internal
and external consumption,

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:23.180
thereby creating an additional
channel for consumption

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:24.180
for your business.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.330
Now, the benefit of
being able to do this

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.530
is your applications that
consume your APIs do not

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:34.490
need to know where those APIs
are actually implemented,

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:37.850
whether they're on premise,
in the cloud, application

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.580
A, application B,
or even versions

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.500
of those same
applications itself.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:46.070
This is very powerful as it
helps our customers create

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:50.690
an ability to then plan what
they want to move and modernize

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.020
into the cloud itself.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.010
So let's take a look
at an example of this.

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:58.790
Today you might start off
with an on-premise data center

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:02.180
where you have existing
workloads itself.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.570
You can now leverage
Google Cloud platform

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:11.540
to either build new
greenfield applications

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.390
or even to even
experiment, right?

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:16.640
You can start with
non-production workloads

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:20.090
or even start looking at things
like cloud bursting, where

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.280
you use Google
Cloud to supplement

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.680
your on-premise resources.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.320
As you get comfortable
with Google Cloud Platform,

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.480
we can then start doing
things like lift and shit,

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.310
as we saw in the
previous slides, where

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.990
we take those existing
on-premise applications

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:37.040
and then deploy them
onto Google Cloud itself,

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:39.620
using something like
Google Compute Engine.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.950
The other strategy, which
was to modernize in place, so

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:45.290
breaking down existing
applications into my core

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.170
services and leveraging
things like Kubernetes

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:52.280
on premise, where you have now
created these microservices

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.800
which can then be
deployed into Kubernetes,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:56.810
which can then be
scaled automatically

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:58.710
through Kubernetes.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:02.090
So with those
modernized microservices

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:05.390
that have been deployed or
developed on Kubernetes itself,

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:09.500
we can then easily migrate
them to Google Cloud Platform,

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:14.910
and more specifically into
Google Kubernetes Engine, GKE.

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:18.260
So at the end of the
day, all these efforts

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.080
are really for the
applications at the front end,

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.700
consuming the APIs itself.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.180
So where we see API
management helping

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:30.740
is sitting between the
two infrastructures,

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:34.430
whether it's on premise or in
the cloud, which then provides

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:37.610
that facade and protects
the consumers from needing

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:43.010
to know specifically where they
need to consume the APIs from.

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:46.360
So with that, what are
some of the challenges

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:49.330
that our customers are
actually experiencing

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:51.760
when they think about
modernization and migration

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.800
to the cloud itself?

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.650
They think about business
continuity, right?

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.960
As they move applications
to the cloud,

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.630
they should not cause
any outage or disruption

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:03.940
to their existing customers.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.190
And this can be
customers from internal

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:08.500
and could be customers from
an external point of view.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.210
They also need to
consider security,

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:14.770
because data inherently
will live in multiple places

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.690
and be accessed in
multiple places as well.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:20.830
You don't want to be
compromised or leave

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.650
APIs open for consumption
without any control

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:26.150
or visibility, which
brings us to visibility.

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:28.060
Without an API
management layer, it

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:30.520
becomes very difficult
to understand

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.789
who your consumers are, be able
to control which applications

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:36.190
are actually
consuming those APIs,

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:39.610
and understand are they
consuming them in a fashion

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:41.090
that you actually want them to--

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:43.539
and then
interoperability as well.

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:46.510
As you lift and shift
certain applications,

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:49.300
you want to maintain
applications

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:52.270
that are traditionally more
familiar with things like SOAP

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:57.130
web services and continue to be
able to offer those things up

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:01.130
in an easy and consumable
fashion itself.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:05.140
And lastly, you want to be able
to handle unmanaged services.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:09.190
Traditionally, as you build out
microservices or applications

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:14.100
itself, how do you actually
understand those consumptions

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:15.330
of those services themself?

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.030
Without some sort of
visibility, without some sort

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:21.480
of API management layer,
it becomes very difficult

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.670
as you scale out.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:25.680
So how can Apigee help?

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.830
So in terms of service
connectivity and helping

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:33.240
business continuity, we can
create that abstraction layer

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:37.740
between GCP and also
on premise itself.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:39.480
We can create an
abstraction layer

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:44.220
between legacy application
and modernized applications

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:45.730
themselves.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:49.560
We hide the complexity of
the application migration

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.260
because your consumers
no longer need

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:57.210
to know where those new services
or legacy services live.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:00.690
They can continue to consume
the way that they're used to.

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:03.120
With APIs management,
you now have the ability

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.170
to also enforce
security consistently

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:08.950
across all your APIs.

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.730
You can also use Apigee
to facilitate things

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:13.945
like mediation between
security protocols.

00:10:13.945 --> 00:10:15.570
And that's something
that we'll discuss

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:20.350
with a particular customer use
case in a couple more minutes.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:21.930
We get great visibility.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:26.310
Because now our traffic is being
proxied through Apigee itself,

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:31.410
we capture every aspect of that
API transaction, where the API

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:34.710
transaction has been called
from, which application,

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:37.560
how often they're actually
consuming the APIs,

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.580
and what sort of
performance and error rates

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:41.580
they're actually seeing.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.460
This gives you great visibility
into understanding your API

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:47.520
program and also
be able to improve,

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:49.320
extend your API program itself.

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:54.600
We give you API facades, where
you can modernize legacy SOAP

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:59.190
applications, turn them
into new RESTful APIs,

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:04.270
and also give you
consistency across the board.

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:08.040
And lastly, service management
across all your microservices

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:12.240
or even your traditional legacy
applications, where you now

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:16.050
have a single platform where
you can enforce policies

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:19.170
across the board, across
all those different services

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:22.770
and then be able to manage
it in a single pane of glass.

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:25.230
One of the solution
benefits in terms

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.240
of being able to
leverage Apigee,

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:30.540
in this particular use
case, is that you're

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:33.720
reducing the risk of that
migration and modernization

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.340
by creating an API facade.

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:38.250
You have the ability to
provide a consistent API

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:42.480
across the board for both
modernized services and legacy

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:43.560
services itself.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.300
At the same time,
you can then start

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:49.500
leveraging innovative
technologies in the cloud,

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:55.440
so start innovating on GCP by
leveraging things like GKE, AI,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:57.210
and now et cetera.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:12:00.480
Where those services
sit shouldn't

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:04.260
matter to the API consumers
themselves or the applications.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:07.350
Apigee will now
mediate and make sure

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:10.410
that it is routed to the
appropriate endpoints.

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:14.310
So with that, I
would like to start

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.390
talking about a specific
customer use case.

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:19.590
And Sarthak was
actually involved

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:22.380
with a particular
customer as he embarked

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:23.813
on this particular journey.

00:12:23.813 --> 00:12:25.230
So with that,
Sarthak, do you want

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:27.570
to introduce the
actual use case itself?

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.060
SARTHAK GANGULY:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:32.970
So this is something I think
I worked on maybe a year or so

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:36.750
ago, maybe a little bit
more than that at this stage

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:38.670
when we started first
working on this.

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:43.260
So this is a retail customer
who has, as you can imagine,

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:47.160
a very standard legacy
on-prem data center.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.140
And like many
other customers who

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:52.640
are looking at that stage, how
can they modernize their stack?

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:54.820
How can they be
more cloud friendly,

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:57.960
take care of capabilities
like cloud bursting

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.640
and horizontal scalability
and things like that?

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:02.820
So when we first
started talking to them,

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:06.090
this is how major portion
of the stack looked like.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.630
So they had this
whole huge legacy,

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:12.480
on-prem, e-commerce
software, which

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.312
was a huge monolith, which
had all the capabilities

00:13:15.312 --> 00:13:16.770
from the customer
management system

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:21.090
to surge to shopping
cart, everything built in.

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:23.760
And then there were-- as
part of the same solution,

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:26.860
they had a bunch of applications
like web apps, mobile apps,

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:31.290
and whatnot, which would
then consume those services

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:33.270
on the back end via APIs.

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:35.790
Keep in mind here, both the
front end and the back end

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:39.210
were part of the
same solution suite.

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:42.510
So this was the current
stage from where we

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:43.825
started our conversation from.

00:13:43.825 --> 00:13:45.450
JOEY WONG: And what
were the challenges

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:47.247
the customer was facing?

00:13:47.247 --> 00:13:49.080
And what were they
trying to solve, I guess,

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:50.710
from this state itself?

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:54.420
SARTHAK GANGULY: So I think
there were two challenges.

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:56.670
One was, I, think that's
the pain point which

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:59.580
they had for a long
time, which was

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:03.240
the front end and the back
end was very tightly coupled.

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.480
And so the thing was
like the front end,

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.880
they have a very different
pace of innovation.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:11.580
They have a very different
technology stack.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.830
There's a very different
mindset of the developers who

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:16.740
are working on an iOS
app versus someone who

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:21.100
is working on the shopping
carts, e-commerce system.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.960
So there was a friction for a
long time about all of that.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:29.040
The front end guys, they
were not able to work faster,

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:30.300
fixing bugs faster.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:33.430
They did not have
enough flexibility.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:37.500
And there was a constant tension
across those different teams

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:40.830
which had been brewing for
many, many years, right?

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:44.490
But I don't think that was
kind of their tipping point.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.197
Their tipping point was
for various reasons.

00:14:47.197 --> 00:14:49.530
A few years back, they started--
a couple of years back,

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:50.310
I think--

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.680
they started seriously
considering cloud.

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:55.200
And they wanted to go
to cloud because things

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:58.920
like they want to do horizontal
scalability for the peak days,

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:03.330
like Black Fridays and whatnot,
and not over provision capacity

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:04.630
for long.

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:07.830
So once they started
looking at that,

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:13.500
then this very tightly coupled
monolithic architecture

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:16.770
became a real
problem, that I think

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:18.570
that was as far as
that tipping point

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:20.100
that, hey, we need
to do something

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:22.740
about this architecture if
we have to actually embark

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.320
on this cloud journey.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.930
JOEY WONG: So in effect, there
were two parts of it, right?

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:31.200
One was the agility and to
be able to innovate quickly.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:32.948
And that was being--

00:15:32.948 --> 00:15:33.990
SARTHAK GANGULY: Correct.

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:36.690
JOEY WONG: Because it was
based on an old architecture,

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:39.842
it was becoming very difficult
for the applications to do so.

00:15:39.842 --> 00:15:40.800
SARTHAK GANGULY: Right.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.342
JOEY WONG: And I
think the second one

00:15:42.342 --> 00:15:44.560
that you mentioned was around
the scalability, right?

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:47.220
SARTHAK GANGULY: Scalability, as
well as all the other benefits

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:48.540
which cloud provides.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:52.690
Once they wanted to
take advantage of that,

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:54.420
then this became a problem.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.760
Scalability, the
horizontal scalability,

00:15:56.760 --> 00:16:00.030
so that no more provisioning,
on-demand scalability,

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:03.120
all the other security
which they get in cloud,

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:04.870
all those things
became a problem.

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:07.690
JOEY WONG: So how did they
actually begin their journey?

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:09.482
SARTHAK GANGULY: So
one of the first things

00:16:09.482 --> 00:16:11.550
they did was they
inserted Apigee

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:14.370
in between those client
and the back end.

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:18.220
So Apigee is a facade, as
Joey mentioned previously.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:22.140
So their idea was that we
already have so those APIs.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:23.820
How to decouple them?

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:26.370
Insert Apigee to
create this facade,

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:30.820
and now suddenly there is
abstraction between what

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:33.630
the clients are consuming,
the client applications are

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:37.170
consuming, and what a
back end is producing.

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:38.850
So now what are
they going to do is

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:40.830
they can change the back end.

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:42.570
And then inside
the Apigee layer,

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:48.420
they can transform the pillars,
the security protocols,

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:51.000
the error codes,
and all those things

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:55.620
to match to what the client
applications actually

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:56.740
are looking for.

00:16:56.740 --> 00:17:01.470
So it becomes the flexibility of
the agility layer, if you may,

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:05.339
using this to decouple that
front-end applications where

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:07.560
the back end is huge monolith.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:08.369
JOEY WONG: Awesome.

00:17:08.369 --> 00:17:12.630
And then what other
things did Apigee provide?

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:16.290
Seeing as we're now proxying the
traffic between the application

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:19.236
and the system, was analytics
something that was important?

00:17:19.236 --> 00:17:20.819
SARTHAK GANGULY:
Yeah, exactly, right?

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:23.099
So because once you
insert Apigee in

00:17:23.099 --> 00:17:25.140
and once you start having
all those API calls,

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:26.970
well, the first
thing they did was

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:28.620
they looked at the Analytics.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:32.137
As a lot of Apigee customers
do because using Analytics,

00:17:32.137 --> 00:17:33.720
they could first--
for the first time,

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:37.290
they could see which one of
the services, which of the APIs

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:39.780
are getting called
at what frequency.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:43.230
What can be considered
as a load-time downtime

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:45.810
to move something over?

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:48.570
Which APIs have the
most problem in terms

00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:51.160
of which is taking the
most highest latency?

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:54.255
Which has the error
rates being higher?

00:17:54.255 --> 00:17:55.680
All those kind of metrics.

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:59.940
And based on that, they decided
their whole business journey

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:03.120
that which of those services
can be migrated when.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:07.590
And so essentially that journey
started from a deep look

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:08.310
at Analytics.

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:11.250
And it took many,
many weeks where

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:13.020
they created many
customer reports

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.140
and looked at many of the
standard reports, which Apigee

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:19.590
provides, to understand all
those aspects from error rates,

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:22.590
latency, traffic patterns.

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:24.330
How does it vary by geography?

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:27.810
How is it varying by
different clients,

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:30.960
like how is it varying by
Android versus iOS versus web.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:32.790
All those things, as
they looked at it,

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:36.630
that's what helped them decide
how they can migrate something

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:37.380
to cloud.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:39.810
JOEY WONG: So even
just by simply

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:42.030
putting Apigee as
the side layer,

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:46.650
as the proxy between the
API requests, the customer

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.230
was able to get deep
Analytics insight

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:52.298
and then use that to help
them plan their path to cloud.

00:18:52.298 --> 00:18:53.340
SARTHAK GANGULY: Exactly.

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:54.840
JOEY WONG: The next
step, obviously,

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:57.343
was then to start breaking down
some of those capabilities.

00:18:57.343 --> 00:18:59.760
SARTHAK GANGULY: Yeah, and
that was the whole idea, right?

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.220
So what they wanted
to do is they wanted

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.737
to refactor this monolith.

00:19:05.737 --> 00:19:07.320
They wanted to get
out of the monolith

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:10.710
and take some of those
individual services

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.830
and put them in GCP, in
Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.450
So in this case, you
can see that they

00:19:15.450 --> 00:19:16.800
have done that with search.

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:18.300
But we would say
that search is also

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:20.580
on prem because
in this stage, I'm

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:24.047
showing the scenario
when they were just

00:19:24.047 --> 00:19:25.630
in the middle of
transitioning, right?

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:27.880
So they have, let's say,
refactored the whole service.

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:30.450
They're running that on
GCP, a brand new service.

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:32.940
But the latency service
is still running on prem

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:36.480
Now they are using Apigee to
first and foremost facilitate

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:38.280
almost like a
blue-green deployment,

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:41.850
so that they route some
traffic to GCP, some traffic

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:42.720
to on prem.

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:44.400
And then, at some
stage when they're

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:46.950
confident that
their cloud service,

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:49.060
their refactored
service is good enough,

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:50.790
that's when they migrate.

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:55.950
Or that's where they route all
those APIs calls to GCP instead

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:58.630
of routing them on prem.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:01.110
But also you can see
the other beauty is now

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:05.340
they can break out an individual
service out of the monolith.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:07.860
And the client applications,
they don't see the impact.

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:09.450
And that's the biggest benefit.

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:11.550
So the client
applications, the guys

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:15.060
who are writing, who
specialize in AngularJS

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:18.810
and writing those web apps, he
doesn't see all these changes

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:20.130
happening behind the scenes.

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:23.070
For him, those clients, this
is completely transparent.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:26.370
Now, as they move
to GCP and they

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.380
build this thing in
Google Kubernetes

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:31.240
Engine, all microservices,
they are writing

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:33.660
new standards of the APIs.

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:38.080
So everything is JSON over REST
and new security standards.

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:42.510
And then Apigee is transforming
all of that to the legacy

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:45.030
or to the-- what is the
standard with the client

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:48.270
applications expect to see?

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:51.010
So that's the biggest advantage
that as you start moving,

00:20:51.010 --> 00:20:54.630
Apigee acts as a blue-green
deployment, a facilitator,

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:55.230
if you may.

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:58.830
And then it provides
all that flexibility

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:03.360
and to basically bring
together the legacy interface

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:05.400
and the modern
interface together.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:06.240
JOEY WONG: Awesome.

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:08.280
So as they progress,
they would then

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:11.735
start deprecating certain things
on premise so by removing,

00:21:11.735 --> 00:21:14.400
say, search from
their on premise

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:17.460
and then start looking, again,
using things like Analytics

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:19.950
to understand what would
be a good candidate

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:21.810
to move into cloud?

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:23.920
SARTHAK GANGULY: Yeah, exactly.

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:25.560
And I mean, now,
in this case, we

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.750
can see that in
this scenario, they

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:30.570
had couple of
services in the Cloud

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:32.520
and some services on prem.

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:35.370
But the front-end
applications, they

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:37.780
don't see all that movement
happening behind the scenes.

00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:39.630
And the beauty is
what we actually

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:42.150
saw-- since now the
front-end developers,

00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:44.820
they had more flexibility,
instead of actually slowing

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:46.320
down, they can
actually went ahead

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:49.080
and they created new
apps on the front end.

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:52.797
Because now they know that,
oh, this is my API definition,

00:21:52.797 --> 00:21:54.630
and this is the API
definition against which

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:55.860
I'm building everything.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:57.720
And this API
definition is not going

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:01.620
to change every time some small
thing changes on the back-end.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:03.580
Previously, that
was a big challenge.

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:06.170
If they do an upgrade
of any of the services,

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:10.110
or of that e-commerce
platform, or they did anything,

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:14.400
there was a big probability that
the front-end apps will break.

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:17.280
And that's where the front-end
developers spent a lot of time

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:19.650
to make sure when all the
back-end things changes,

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:21.120
their things did not break.

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:24.570
JOEY WONG: So by creating
an API-first strategy, that

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:29.160
gave the customer an ability
to innovate and create new APIs

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:33.360
and services and then be able
to implement them using whatever

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:34.800
technology they choose to.

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:36.175
SARTHAK GANGULY:
And in new apps.

00:22:36.175 --> 00:22:39.750
I mean, they were kind of stuck
at having those Android and iOS

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:42.390
apps at a pretty legacy
technology for a while

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:46.980
because they just did not have
time other than firefighting,

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:48.690
other than to comply
with the back end

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:52.110
because it is the same engine,
at the end of the day, which

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:54.750
is supporting their
stores as well

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.490
as their e-commerce engine.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:58.830
So there were requirements
from all sides, which

00:22:58.830 --> 00:23:01.890
will shape that back end,
which the front-end guys had

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:03.060
no control on.

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:05.140
They were always with
their reactionary mode

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:09.030
so that left their mobile
apps and their web apps

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:11.520
in a pretty dire state.

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:13.110
It was pretty legacy technology.

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:17.010
They did not have
native Android app.

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:19.170
And there were a
need for a couple

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:22.710
of other modern applications,
like for their B2B scenarios

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:24.050
and things like that.

00:23:24.050 --> 00:23:27.840
So finally, while all this
back-end mayhem, if you may,

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:29.730
is going on, that's
when they were

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:34.410
able to roll up their sleeves
and build some new applications

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:37.920
and improve and modernize
their existing applications.

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:38.880
JOEY WONG: Awesome.

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:42.360
And then, inherently, a
question that always comes up

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:45.630
is by introducing
another layer is--

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:48.420
what sort of performance
challenges, or if any,

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:49.392
were faced?

00:23:49.392 --> 00:23:51.600
SARTHAK GANGULY: Are you
talking in terms of latency?

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:53.490
JOEY WONG: Yeah, latency.

00:23:53.490 --> 00:23:57.013
SARTHAK GANGULY: Yeah, so I
don't think we showed here.

00:23:57.013 --> 00:23:58.680
But as you can imagine,
with the retail,

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:03.570
there are a lot of
services, like catalogs,

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:05.830
and customer data, and--

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:07.200
JOEY WONG: Store locations.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:10.330
SARTHAK GANGULY: --store
location, and all of that data.

00:24:10.330 --> 00:24:11.880
All those things are cacheable.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:15.180
And Apigee has a very powerful
caching layer with level one

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:16.860
and level two cache built in.

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:21.060
Wherever that was possible, they
actually used the Apigee cache,

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:25.110
and that actually reduced their
latency quite drastically.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:30.690
Now, in some other cases,
which cannot be cached,

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:32.310
which are real
time, for example,

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:36.300
like your shopping cart or,
let's say, recommendation,

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:39.060
because recommendations
constantly change,

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:41.570
those things, whether
there was a latency add--

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:43.560
yeah, maybe.

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:45.540
But keep in mind,
in those cases,

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:47.440
we're looking at
APIs which are taking

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:50.250
a few seconds from the
app to the back end

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:51.780
to do all the stuff and go back.

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:53.760
And then Apigee's
latency add was,

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:57.090
like, single digit in
milliseconds or double-digit

00:24:57.090 --> 00:25:02.320
milliseconds, so like 20, 30
milliseconds-- in that range.

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:06.150
And given the amount of
benefits, which we are getting,

00:25:06.150 --> 00:25:10.470
a 30-millisecond bump
compared to a three second

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:12.520
was not considered much.

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:14.280
And obviously,
there are a huge set

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:16.470
of APIs, huge set of
services for which there

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:18.990
was a huge, huge
performance bump wherever

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:23.340
they could use the caching
policy, which is built in.

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:27.570
One other thing I might add
is although it might not

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:30.540
look obvious, as you are
going through the journey,

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:33.750
we started off with a
lot of heavy APIs, which

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:34.980
were SOAP, which were XMLA.

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:36.480
As we are going
through the journey,

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:39.280
the APIs were a lot lighter.

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:42.150
They were JSON over REST.

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:44.950
We are liking lightweight
microservices.

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:47.460
So as part of the
overall journey,

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:50.398
the [INAUDIBLE] or the
latency of the APIs--

00:25:50.398 --> 00:25:51.815
JOEY WONG: Actually
became better.

00:25:51.815 --> 00:25:53.398
SARTHAK GANGULY:
--became a lot better

00:25:53.398 --> 00:25:54.690
than what it was previously.

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:55.620
JOEY WONG: Awesome.

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:59.070
And then I can imagine as
well with the caching, that's

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:03.930
an interesting use case because
now, if you're returning

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:07.890
a response from the cache
closer to the application that's

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:11.140
consuming the APIs,
you could potentially

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:15.070
be servicing more transactions
because you're not necessarily

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:17.162
hitting the back-end system.

00:26:17.162 --> 00:26:19.495
SARTHAK GANGULY: So yeah,
that's actually a great point.

00:26:19.495 --> 00:26:23.260
So one of the challenges
they had was--

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:26.020
so the back end, when the
e-commerce engine was built

00:26:26.020 --> 00:26:28.090
and that whole
infrastructure was built,

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:31.390
that was built
thinking of the world

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:34.990
with a browser-first
approach, web word.

00:26:34.990 --> 00:26:37.160
Which was like, oh, this
can have, let's say,

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:40.540
whatever, x number of
calls simultaneously,

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:43.030
10x number of calls
a day or whatnot.

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:46.270
They did not think inherently
the mobile-first approach

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:50.080
and the scale which
mobile brings in.

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:52.660
And they had severe
problems with lot

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:56.680
of outages at various different
times, slow performance,

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:59.860
and things like that,
especially during peak hours.

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:02.150
They had all of that.

00:27:02.150 --> 00:27:05.460
So as they were able
to cache in Apigee

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:08.720
using the L1 and L2 layer,
a lot of those performance

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:12.790
worries, at least for some of
those services like shopping--

00:27:12.790 --> 00:27:16.060
sorry, like product
catalogs and all, those

00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:17.170
were drastically reduced.

00:27:17.170 --> 00:27:19.900
JOEY WONG: Yeah, I can
imagine in today's world even

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:22.625
with Google Assistant,
at Google Homes, yeah,

00:27:22.625 --> 00:27:25.000
that's another avenue, another
channel that's flowing up.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:26.042
SARTHAK GANGULY: Exactly.

00:27:26.042 --> 00:27:29.900
No one thought of those
avenues five years back.

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:30.760
JOEY WONG: Yeah.

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:34.310
So in this particular
diagram in the final state,

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:37.750
there's an interesting thing
here where you actually--

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:40.572
the customer's actually
operating in both Google Cloud

00:27:40.572 --> 00:27:42.280
and in their own
traditional data center.

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:43.360
SARTHAK GANGULY: Correct.

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:45.430
JOEY WONG: Can you share
with us some of the--

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:50.140
SARTHAK GANGULY: Yeah, so
what happened is, at the end

00:27:50.140 --> 00:27:55.120
of the day, they decided that
they will migrat and rewrite

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:58.240
and whatnot most of the
applications in cloud,

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:01.475
but they were not going
to rewrite shopping cart.

00:28:01.475 --> 00:28:03.850
Shopping cart, they are going
to maintain in the existing

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:06.340
e-commerce solution on
prem because it is a very

00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:07.840
complex piece of software.

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:11.270
And they did not see any
business value by rewriting it.

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:14.890
So as they were migrating-- and
we talked about in the previous

00:28:14.890 --> 00:28:15.390
slides--

00:28:15.390 --> 00:28:19.180
Apigee was the layer
behind which they migrated.

00:28:19.180 --> 00:28:20.980
But not only that,
Apigee was the layer

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:25.450
which created the hybrid
and the final state, when--

00:28:25.450 --> 00:28:29.020
so their final steady state
will be they have their shopping

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:30.520
cart application
on prem and they

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:32.980
have all the other
applications as microservices

00:28:32.980 --> 00:28:34.750
and whatnot in GCP.

00:28:34.750 --> 00:28:38.770
And then Apigee sits in front,
which then routes traffic

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:44.680
to either GCP or to shopping
cart based on different logics

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:45.910
put inside Apigee.

00:28:45.910 --> 00:28:47.950
So this is actually
Apigee helping

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:52.540
in creating a hybrid state,
built in on prem and Cloud,

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:54.320
using an API-first approach.

00:28:54.320 --> 00:28:56.080
So normally when
you think of hybrid,

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:59.440
customers start thinking in
terms of, oh, I need to do--

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:00.820
I need to set up a VPN.

00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:02.110
I need to set up a VPC.

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:05.210
I need to set up an interconnect
and all of those kind of things

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:06.400
to do hybrid.

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:08.890
Yes, that would be
true in some scenarios.

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:12.430
In other scenarios, if you
do an API-first paradigm,

00:29:12.430 --> 00:29:15.040
if you do an API-first
design for applications,

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:16.875
you can do it at a
hybrid like this.

00:29:16.875 --> 00:29:18.250
And this is
something we actually

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:23.350
see customers do quite often,
not only just in one scenario.

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:24.870
We have many, many
customers who are

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:29.110
doing this kind of
hybrid deployments.

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:31.830
JOEY WONG: That's
an amazing story.

00:29:31.830 --> 00:29:35.020
I believe this was actually
done in a quite a short period

00:29:35.020 --> 00:29:37.060
of time as well.

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:40.390
SARTHAK GANGULY: Yeah,
I mean the overall--

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:43.900
I honestly don't have the
whole time frame in mind.

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:45.790
But the initial thing--

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:49.600
the initial proof of concept,
the initial first migration

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:52.060
of the application and
whatnot was achieved

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:54.170
within just a few months.

00:29:54.170 --> 00:29:58.090
Now, as you can imagine, there
are all these different pieces.

00:29:58.090 --> 00:29:58.930
That needs to moved.

00:29:58.930 --> 00:30:02.760
That needs to be rewritten,
and tested, and run.

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:04.690
So that's a--

00:30:04.690 --> 00:30:06.370
I mean, I wouldn't
trivialize it.

00:30:06.370 --> 00:30:08.080
That's a pretty decent project.

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:10.270
But to proof out this
concept and to see

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:13.870
if we can take away one service
out and rewrite it, run it

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:17.980
on Google Kubernetes
Engine, plug that to Apigee,

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:21.740
and then expose a set of
APIs, a coherent set of APIs

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:23.800
to all the client
applications, that

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:26.280
was done in actually a
very short period of time,

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:27.730
in like a couple of months.

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:30.337
I think it's like a
little more than a month.

00:30:30.337 --> 00:30:31.920
And that actually
amazed our customer.

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:33.220
They did not expect--

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:35.890
JOEY WONG: Yeah, I mean, not
meaning to trivialize it,

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:37.240
but I'm just--

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:40.630
I guess the question is,
how much longer would it

00:30:40.630 --> 00:30:41.630
have taken?

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:42.610
How much more complex
would it have been?

00:30:42.610 --> 00:30:43.210
SARTHAK GANGULY: I
mean, they didn't

00:30:43.210 --> 00:30:45.090
do this for so many years.

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.140
I mean, they had
this e-commerce,

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:49.780
this on-prem monolith
application for so many years.

00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:51.250
They couldn't do it.

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:53.110
I mean, it's not
that they just--

00:30:53.110 --> 00:30:55.472
it was the first time they
thought about doing this.

00:30:55.472 --> 00:30:57.430
They have thinking about
doing this for a while

00:30:57.430 --> 00:31:00.310
because they had all these
challenges for a while.

00:31:00.310 --> 00:31:04.900
So I mean, without having
this layer as Apigee, Apigee

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:07.390
at that layer, they
couldn't even achieve that.

00:31:07.390 --> 00:31:08.890
JOEY WONG: They
couldn't achieve it.

00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:10.890
They couldn't even think
about taking that task.

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:12.920
SARTHAK GANGULY:
How to do it, yeah.

00:31:12.920 --> 00:31:15.620
JOEY WONG: Amazing, all right.

00:31:15.620 --> 00:31:17.210
Thank you for
sharing that with us.

00:31:17.210 --> 00:31:18.080
SARTHAK GANGULY:
Yeah, absolutely.

